        "original_id": "f3ebb75d-b6b7-574f-a1d5-a47f538a8f04",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Newark, Nottinghamshire, England\n- Organizations: UK Parliament, British House of Commons, Conservative Party, Labour Party, Standards Committee\n- People: Robert Jenrick, Patrick Mercer, Fiona Jones\n- Events: 1997 general election, 2001 general election, Newark by-election 2014\n- Roles: Member of Parliament, Independent MP\n\nFacts:\n- Fiona Jones was elected as Member of Parliament for Newark in Labour's landslide victory in the 1997 general election.\n- Patrick Mercer was elected as a Conservative Member of Parliament in the 2001 general election.\n- Patrick Mercer represented the constituency of Newark in Parliament.\n- Patrick Mercer resigned the Conservative Party parliamentary whip in May 2013 following questions about paid advocacy.\n- Patrick Mercer then sat as an Independent Member of Parliament for Newark until the end of April 2014.\n- Patrick Mercer resigned at the end of April 2014 after the Standards Committee suspended him for six months for a sustained and pervasive breach of the House's rules.\n- Newark is a UK Parliament constituency in Nottinghamshire, England.\n- The Newark by-election was held in the Newark constituency of the British House of Commons on 5 June 2014 following Patrick Mercer's resignation.\n- Robert Jenrick of the Conservative Party won the Newark by-election on 5 June 2014 and is the current representative for Newark.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "a399497e-a719-57e6-a20a-29936c86230a",
        "text": "Page: Newark (UK Parliament constituency). Sentence: Newark is a constituency in Nottinghamshire, England.\nPage: Newark (UK Parliament constituency). Sentence: It is currently represented by Robert Jenrick of the Conservative Party, who won the seat in a by-election on 5 June 2014, following the resignation of Patrick Mercer in April 2014.\nPage: Patrick Mercer. Sentence: He was elected as a Conservative in the 2001 general election, until resigning the party's parliamentary whip in May 2013 following questions surrounding paid advocacy, and was an Independent MP representing the constituency of Newark in Parliament until his resignation at the end of April 2014 after the Standards Committee suspended him for six months for \"sustained and pervasive breach of the house's rules\".\nPage: Fiona Jones. Sentence: She was elected as a Member of Parliament (MP) for Newark in Labour's landslide victory in the 1997 general election.\nPage: Newark by-election, 2014. Sentence: The Newark by-election was a by-election in the Newark constituency of the British House of Commons, which was held on 5 June 2014, following the resignation of Patrick Mercer.",
